Short:        Two-pane file manager for Kickstart 1.3 and up
Author:       Paul Seibert (Desert Palms Software)
Uploader:     Paul Seibert <your@email.here>
Type:         util/dir
Version:      1.0
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 1.3
Distribution: Aminet
Requires:     Nothing extra. Optional: LhA (for extracting and packing).

Oasis 1.0 - (c) 2026 Desert Palms Software. Written by Paul Seibert.
Freeware.

Oasis is a two-pane file manager in the classic style: a source pane and a
destination pane side by side, a bank of buttons below, and everything
done from one window. It is written in C for AmigaOS 1.3 (V33) and runs
unchanged on 2.x and 3.x. It only uses the 1.3 operating system, so it
works on a stock A500 with 512K as well as on bigger machines, and it
looks at home on both the 1.3 and the 2.0+ Workbench.

Besides copying files, Oasis looks at them (text, hex, IFF pictures and
sounds), looks inside LhA archives without needing LhA, and turns floppy
disks into ADF images and back.

Browsing
--------

 * Two panes, each with a path field, a scroller and a status line (files,
   drawers, selection size, free space). The active pane has the orange
   frame; Tab switches. The info line shows the entry under the cursor:
   what kind of file it is, size, date, protection bits and comment.
 * Drive buttons for the drives you have, plus Devs (all devices, volumes
   and assigns), Parent, Root and Rescan. Disk changes are noticed.
 * Click to select, double-click or Return to open. Cursor keys, Shift for
   a page, Space selects, Backspace goes up, Del deletes.
 * Sort by name, size or date (drawers first); hide .info files.

File operations
---------------

 * Copy, Move, Rename, Delete, MakeDir. Copy and Delete work through whole
   drawers. Icons travel with their files: copying "foo" copies "foo.info".
 * Comments, protection bits and dates are kept. Dates are kept on 1.3 too
   (Oasis sets them with a DOS packet, as 1.3 has no SetFileDate).
 * Moves on the same disk are renames; across disks Oasis copies, then
   deletes the original only if everything arrived.
 * A progress bar, and Esc stops cleanly (never half a file left).
 * Clear requesters: Retry / Skip / Skip all / Abort, Replace / Replace all,
   Unprotect for delete-protected files. Deleting always asks first.
   Copying a drawer into itself is refused.

Tools
-----

 * Protect: the eight hsparwed bits as toggle buttons.
 * Comment: edit a file comment.
 * Size: adds up drawers; their size then shows in the list.
 * Search: by name below the current drawer, with AmigaDOS wildcards
   (#?  ?  *  (a|b)) or just part of a name. The results fill the pane;
   copy or delete them directly, or double-click one to go there.
 * Compare: selects what is only on one side, or differs in size or date.
 * Custom buttons: up to 12, defined in a plain text file, with the
   selected files (%s, %f), this drawer (%p) and the other pane (%d).

Viewers
-------

 * Text viewer: long lines wrap, tabs line up, ANSI colour codes in
   readmes are hidden. Tab switches to hex.
 * Hex viewer: any size of file.
 * IFF ILBM pictures on their own screen: lores and hires, interlace, up
   to 32 colours, Extra Halfbrite and HAM. Click to return.
 * IFF 8SVX sounds, plain or Fibonacci-packed, mono or stereo, played
   through audio.device with little chip RAM. Esc stops.

LhA archives
------------

 * Return on an .lha shows what is in it: sizes, packing, dates, drawers
   and file comments. This is built into Oasis and works without LhA.
 * The LhA button extracts archives into the other pane, or packs the
   selection (with its icons) into a new archive there. This runs the LhA
   program, which is shareware and not included: put it in C:, or name it
   in the prefs file.

ADF disk images
---------------

 * Write an .adf onto a floppy in DF0:-DF3:, with or without verifying
   each track. Oasis names the disk it is about to replace and asks first.
 * Read a floppy into a new .adf in the other pane. Unreadable tracks are
   saved as zeros and reported.
 * Write-protected disks, empty drives, images of the wrong size, and
   images on the disk being imaged are refused. Esc stops between tracks.
 * 880K (DD) disks only.

Installing
----------

Copy the Oasis drawer anywhere you like. Oasis needs no libraries, fonts
or assigns beyond the ROM. Its settings live in S:Oasis.prefs, or in
Oasis.prefs next to Oasis: an example is in the drawer, with comments
explaining every line. Project > Reload prefs applies changes.

On Kickstart 1.3, custom buttons and LhA need C:Run, which every
Workbench disk has.

There is also a disk image, oasis.adf, on the GitHub page: it boots
straight into Oasis.

Command mode
------------

"oasis CMD" runs Oasis's engine from the CLI or a script, without the
window: LIST, TREE, COPY, MOVE, DELETE, MAKEDIR, RENAME, SIZE, SEARCH,
PROTECT, COMMENT, COMPARE, TYPE, LHALIST, ADFREAD, ADFWRITE and more.
"oasis CMD" alone lists them all. For example:

    oasis CMD COPY Work:Projects TO DF1:
    oasis CMD SEARCH Work: #?.mod
    oasis CMD ADFWRITE RAM:game.adf DF0: VERIFY

Known limits
------------

 * ADF imaging is for 880K DD floppies (HD disks need OS 2.0 or later).
 * 8SVX: plain and Fibonacci-delta samples (the common kinds).
 * ILBM: what the original chipset shows (up to 64 colours with EHB, or
   HAM6); AGA pictures are recognised but not shown.
 * Extracting and packing need the LhA program.

Technical notes
---------------

 * Written in C, cross-compiled with GCC 6.5 (bebbo's amiga-gcc) against
   the Kickstart 1.3 includes, so it cannot call anything newer by
   accident. About 100K of 68000 code.
 * The interface is built by hand from plain Intuition gadgets (1.3 has
   no GadTools or ASL), with its own requesters.
 * Tested under WinUAE on Kickstart 1.3 (A500, OCS, 512K + 512K) and
   Kickstart 3.1 (ECS, 1MB + 1MB) with automated test disks: copies are
   compared byte for byte, dates and protection bits checked, decoded
   pictures checked against their source, sound playing times measured,
   ADF images written and read back byte for byte, and free memory
   measured before and after. No memory is lost.

